namespace mem;

/**
 * Memory: raw heap allocation, and reading the reference counts.
 *
 * Nothing here frees anything for you. Every `mem::alloc` needs a matching `mem::free`, and
 * using memory after freeing it is undefined. See book/concept/pointers_and_refs_v2.md.
 *
 * Counts are always counts of *elements*, never bytes. The byte-level functions below are the
 * single exception, and the only place a size in bytes appears at all.
 *
 * `mem` sits beside `core`'s root-namespace types rather than under `std`, because none of this is a
 * utility you opt into - it is how the language's own types are built, and `array` and `string` in this
 * same directory are written against it. What keeps it out of the root is only that you do not write
 * it every line.
 */

/**
 * The raw allocator, answered by the compiler.
 *
 * Builtins rather than `extern malloc`/`realloc`/`free`, and the reason is one the language needed
 * rather than a preference. Two places allocated - these, and the runtime's own block for a class
 * or a closure - and because nothing saw both, "how much memory does this program still hold" had
 * no answer at all. Routing these through the compiler gives the two one owner and one counter,
 * which is what `live_allocations` below reads.
 *
 * `alloc`, `realloc` and `free` beneath them are still the choke point the rest of the namespace
 * funnels through, so a different allocator - an arena, a pool - is still a change to three
 * function bodies. What moved is only where the bytes underneath those three come from.
 *
 * Nothing outside this file should call them. `usize` stands in for C's `size_t`.
 */
#[builtin: "alloc_bytes"]
function alloc_bytes(usize $bytes) : ptr<uint8>;

#[builtin: "realloc_bytes"]
function realloc_bytes(ptr<uint8> $p, usize $bytes) : ptr<uint8>;

#[builtin: "free_bytes"]
function free_bytes(ptr<uint8> $p) : void;

/**
 * The two byte-level operations that move memory around without ever asking for any.
 *
 * Still bound straight to C, because there is nothing about them the compiler needs to know: they
 * neither take a block out of the allocator nor give one back, so they are invisible to the count.
 *
 * `int32` stands in for C's `int`; it agrees on every target currently supported.
 */
extern {
    function memcpy as copy_bytes(ptr<uint8> $dst, ptr<uint8> $src, usize $bytes) : ptr<uint8>;
    function memset as set_bytes(ptr<uint8> $p, int32 $value, usize $bytes) : ptr<uint8>;
}

/**
 * The size of one `T` in bytes, as a compile-time constant.
 *
 * This is the allocation stride, so it includes any tail padding - `size_of<T>() * $n` is exactly
 * the room `$n` elements of `T` need, and `$p:$[n]` lands on element `n`.
 */
#[builtin: "size_of"]
function size_of<T>() : usize;

/**
 * The alignment `T` requires, in bytes, as a compile-time constant.
 */
#[builtin: "align_of"]
function align_of<T>() : usize;

/**
 * True when duplicating a `T` is duplicating its bytes and nothing else, as a compile-time constant.
 *
 * False for anything a copy has to *arrange*: a class or an interface, where a copy is one more
 * reference; a type whose author wrote a copy constructor; a struct holding any of those, however
 * deep. The compiler decides this for every copy it inserts anyway - this is that same answer, said
 * out loud, so a container can be written against it instead of assuming one way or the other.
 *
 * What it is for: a buffer of `T` can be duplicated with one `mem::copy` exactly when this is true,
 * and has to be copied element by element when it is not. `array<T>` branches on it, and the two arms
 * are both correct for every `T` - the branch buys back the `memcpy`, it does not decide correctness.
 *
 * Write the element-wise arm anyway. This says a bulk copy is *allowed*, never that a per-element one
 * would be wrong.
 */
#[builtin: "is_trivially_copyable"]
function is_trivially_copyable<T>() : bool;

/**
 * True when a `T` going out of scope has something to give back, as a compile-time constant.
 *
 * The teardown counterpart of `is_trivially_copyable`, and the same kind of answer: true for a class
 * or an interface, for a type whose author wrote a destructor, and for a struct holding any of those.
 * False for a primitive, for a pointer - an address says nothing about what is behind it - and for a
 * struct made only of those.
 *
 * What it is for: a container holding raw storage has to destroy its elements before it frees the
 * block, and there is nothing to do when this is false. Guarding the loop with it is what keeps an
 * `array<int32>`'s destructor free rather than a walk over `count()` elements that each do nothing.
 */
#[builtin: "needs_destruction"]
function needs_destruction<T>() : bool;

/**
 * The value at `$place`, handed over. **The storage keeps its bytes and stops being an owner.**
 *
 * The read that *ends* a claim rather than duplicating it. `$x = $buffer:$[3]` copies - a `string`
 * element gets its retain, and the slot still names the buffer it named before, so something has to
 * release it later. This does not copy: what comes back is the only owner, and whatever was managing
 * that storage is now managing storage that holds nothing.
 *
 * Which is why it is here, in the namespace where `free` lives, and not a keyword. **Nothing checks
 * this for you.** Take twice from one place and two owners release one value, exactly as freeing
 * twice frees one block twice. It is for the code that already accounts for its own storage - a
 * container whose `count()` says which slots are live - and for nothing else.
 *
 * `mv` is what you want for a variable, and this refuses one: a local's scope still owes it a
 * teardown, so emptying it here would destroy the value twice. A temporary is refused for the mirror
 * reason - it owns its value already and has no storage to empty.
 *
 * `array<T>` is written on this three times: its destructor and `clear()` take each live element and
 * let it fall out of scope, and `pop()` takes the last one and hands it to the caller. Without it
 * `pop()` could only *copy* the element out, leaving the slot holding a second owner that nothing
 * would ever release.
 */
#[builtin: "take"]
function take<T>(T& $place) : T;

/**
 * Allocates room for `$count` elements of `T`, uninitialized.
 *
 * Returns `null` when the allocation fails. Because the result is `ptr<T>` and not `T&`, the type
 * system makes you acknowledge that before you can use it as a reference.
 */
function alloc<T>(usize $count) : ptr<T>
{
    return ptr<T>(alloc_bytes($count * size_of<T>()));
}

/**
 * Resizes an allocation to `$count` elements, preserving as much of the contents as fits.
 *
 * May move the block, so the returned address is the one to keep - the old one is dead. Does not
 * preserve any alignment stronger than the allocator's own.
 */
function realloc<T>(ptr<T> $p, usize $count) : ptr<T>
{
    return ptr<T>(realloc_bytes(ptr<uint8>($p:$), $count * size_of<T>()));
}

/**
 * Releases an allocation. Freeing `null` is a no-op.
 */
function free<T>(ptr<T> $p) : void
{
    free_bytes(ptr<uint8>($p:$));
}

/**
 * Copies `$count` elements from `$src` to `$dst`. The two regions must not overlap.
 */
function copy<T>(ptr<T> $dst, ptr<T> $src, usize $count) : void
{
    copy_bytes(ptr<uint8>($dst:$), ptr<uint8>($src:$), $count * size_of<T>());
}

/**
 * Fills `$count` elements with zero bytes.
 */
function zero<T>(ptr<T> $p, usize $count) : void
{
    set_bytes(ptr<uint8>($p:$), 0, $count * size_of<T>());
}

/**
 * How many references name the object `$handle` holds. Zero for a null handle.
 *
 * Here beside `alloc` rather than at the root, because it asks about the same thing they do: a
 * reference count is a word inside the heap block these functions hand out. "May I write to this"
 * is a memory question. See book/concept/ownership_and_moving.md.
 *
 * A compiler builtin rather than a library function, because the count is a word inside the heap
 * block - `{ i64 __strong, i64 __weak, ptr __typeinfo, %Foo }` - and no Echo spelling reaches it.
 * Nothing links against this and it has no symbol of its own.
 *
 * The parameter is a **borrow**, not a value. A by-value class parameter is one more reference, so
 * a version taking `T` would answer one too high at every call - which is exactly the question the
 * caller is asking.
 *
 * Zero for null is not a degenerate answer, it is the useful one. It lets a copy-on-write check
 * read as a single condition: static bytes that nobody owns are not *uniquely* owned either, so
 * they have to be copied before they are written, exactly as shared bytes do. `string` is written
 * against precisely that - see `stdlib/core/string.eco`.
 */
#[builtin: "ref_count"]
function ref_count<T>(T& $handle) : usize;

/**
 * How many handles need the *block* `$handle` names to stay readable. Zero for a null handle.
 *
 * The other count in the same header, and it answers a different question. `ref_count` is about the
 * object: at zero the destructor has run and the object is gone. This one is about the memory: at
 * zero the block itself is given back. They are not the same moment, and that gap is exactly what a
 * `weak<T>` lives in - it keeps the block readable so it can ask whether the object is still there,
 * instead of dangling to find out.
 *
 * All the strong references together hold **one** of these, seated with the first and given back by
 * the release that takes `ref_count` to zero. So a freshly built object reads 1 here, and each
 * `weak<T>` naming it adds one more.
 *
 * Mostly for tests. Nothing observable from Echo distinguishes a balanced pair of counts from a
 * leaked one, so this is how the corpus pins a reference cycle actually being collected rather
 * than merely appearing to be - see `tests_eco/classes/weak_cycle_no_leak.eco`.
 */
#[builtin: "weak_count"]
function weak_count<T>(T& $handle) : usize;

/**
 * How many allocations the program is still holding.
 *
 * A count of *allocations* - not of elements, and not of bytes. `alloc` and a successful growing
 * `realloc` of nothing move it up by one, `free` and a `realloc` down to zero size move it down by
 * one, and everything else leaves it alone: freeing `null`, resizing a block that already exists,
 * and a `realloc` the allocator refused. That last one matters more than it looks - the block is
 * still yours after a failure, so counting it as freed would leave every later reading one too low.
 *
 * The runtime's own blocks are in here too. A class, a closure's captured environment and an
 * `array<T>`'s storage are all allocations, so the number covers the whole program rather than the
 * part written against this namespace.
 *
 * Needs `--track-allocations` (or `--explain-memory`, which implies it). Without it there is no
 * counter, and rather than answer zero - the one wrong answer you could not tell from the right one -
 * the compiler refuses the call where you wrote it.
 *
 * This is `weak_count`'s argument one level down, and the same job: nothing else observable from
 * Echo tells a program that gave everything back apart from one that did not. So it is how the
 * corpus asserts the absence of a leak instead of inferring it from destructor output.
 *
 * **Read it after a scope, not at the end of a file.** A variable declared at module scope is
 * released after the last statement, so a check written there runs while its owners are still alive:
 *
 * ```
 * {
 *     Node $n = Node(1);
 *     assert(mem::live_allocations() > 0);
 * }
 * assert(mem::live_allocations() == 0);
 * ```
 */
#[builtin: "live_allocations"]
function live_allocations() : usize;
